WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:07.260
This is a very famous cautionary tale in math&nbsp;
known as Moser's Circle Problem. Some of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.860
may have seen this before but what I want to&nbsp;
do here is barely explain what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.860
The way this starts is we take a circle and put&nbsp;
two points on that circle and connect them with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.140
line, that is a chord of the circle and note that&nbsp;
it divides the circle into two different regions.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:27.600
If I add a third point and then connect that to&nbsp;
the previous two points with two more chords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.460
then these lines all divide the circle&nbsp;
into four separate regions. Then if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.780
add a fourth point and connect that to the&nbsp;
previous three and you play the same game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.100
you count up how many regions has this cut&nbsp;
the circle into, you end up with eight.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.560
Add a fifth point to the circle, connect it to&nbsp;
the previous four, count up the total number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.420
regions and if you're careful with your counting,&nbsp;
you'll get a total of sixteen. Naturally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.380
you can guess what might come next but would&nbsp;
you bet your life on it? Add a sixth point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.700
connect it to all the previous ones and if you&nbsp;
carefully count up all the different regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:04.440
you end up not with the power of two you&nbsp;
might have expected but just one shy of it.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.140
Some of you might be raising your hands saying,&nbsp;
"doesn't it depend on where we put the points?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.000
For example, watch how this middle region&nbsp;
disappears if I place everything nice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.540
symmetrically around the circle. So,&nbsp;
yes, it does depend but we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.140
consider the cases where you never have&nbsp;
any three lines intersecting with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.920
other. This would be the generic case&nbsp;
if you just choose "n" random points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.480
almost certainly you'll never have three lines&nbsp;
coincide but setting aside the technical nuances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.980
the problem is such a tease. It looks so&nbsp;
convincingly like powers of two until it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:40.080
barely breaks and I have such a strange soft spot&nbsp;
for this particular question. When I was younger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:45.240
I wrote a poem about it and also a song and on the&nbsp;
one hand, it's kind of silly because this is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.560
one example of what the mathematician Richard&nbsp;
Guy called The Strong Law of Small Numbers.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.060
Summed up in the phrase, there aren't enough small&nbsp;
numbers to meet the many demands made of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.680
But I think what I really like about this&nbsp;
problem is that if you sit down to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.340
work out what is the real pattern,&nbsp;
what's actually going on here - one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.240
it's just a really good exercise and problem&nbsp;
solving, so, it makes for a nice lesson right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.740
here. But also, it's not just a coincidence&nbsp;
that it starts off being powers of two.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:15.000
There's a very good reason this happens.&nbsp;
And it's also not a coincidence that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.960
seemingly randomly hit another power of two&nbsp;
a little bit later on the tenth iteration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.260
So, we've got this pattern and what you&nbsp;
want to find is what function describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.580
it. If you put N points on the boundary of&nbsp;
a circle and you connect them with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:36.000
possible chords and you count how many&nbsp;
regions the circle has been cut into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.620
if the answer isn't a power of two, what is&nbsp;
it? What function of N should we plug in?

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.720
As always with math, problem solving&nbsp;
rule number one if you're stuck is to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:50.160
solving easier questions somehow related&nbsp;
to the problem at hand. It helps you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.240
a foothold and sometimes those answers are&nbsp;
helpful in the final question. In this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.440
two warm up questions that come to mind&nbsp;
are how many total chords are there in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.480
this diagram and at how many points within the&nbsp;
circle do those chords intersect each other?

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.200
The first question is relatively&nbsp;
friendly. Every one of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.740
chords corresponds uniquely with&nbsp;
a pair of points on the circle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.780
So, effectively what you want to do is count&nbsp;
how many distinct pairs of points are there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.060
There's a function that does this, it's called&nbsp;
"n choose 2". By definition, this counts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.340
number of distinct pairs you can choose from&nbsp;
a set of N items where order doesn't matter.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.080
To calculate it, the way you often think about&nbsp;
it is that you have N choices for what your first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.060
item should be and then you have N minus one&nbsp;
options for what that second item should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.400
But simply multiplying those would&nbsp;
overcount. Since for a given pair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.140
you would have two distinct ways to arrive at that&nbsp;
pair. And remember, we don't care about order.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.120
To account for this, you divide by two.&nbsp;
So for example, seven choose two would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:57.000
look like seven times six divided by two,&nbsp;
which is seven times three or 21. And if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.500
you count up the number of distinct pairs&nbsp;
of 7 items, there are indeed 21 of them.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:07.920
Counting the number of intersection points in&nbsp;
the diagram is a little bit trickier. One idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.320
might be that it should be the number of pairs&nbsp;
of chords since every intersection point comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:18.300
from two different chords. However, that would&nbsp;
not quite be right because the association is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.260
not unique. You can find a pair of chords&nbsp;
that don't intersect within the circle.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.100
As I said, it's a little bit tricky.&nbsp;
I'd encourage you to try to pause and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.000
think about it for yourself and if you&nbsp;
do that, you give yourself a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.120
Maybe if you're a little bit lucky,&nbsp;
here's one thing you might notice;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:39.060
every intersection point is uniquely associated&nbsp;
with a quadruplet of points on the exterior.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.620
For a given quadruplet, you look at the&nbsp;
two kind of diagonal cords between them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.580
and those will intersect within the circle&nbsp;
and it goes the other way around. Every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.820
intersection point corresponds&nbsp;
with some quadruplet of points.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:58.680
So, what you want now is to count how many&nbsp;
distinct ways can you choose four items given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:03.840
N total choices. This is very similar to the&nbsp;
previous question. The function that answers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.180
it is called "n choose 4" which by definition&nbsp;
counts the number of quadruplets from a set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.040
of size N and the way to calculate it is similar&nbsp;
to what we saw before. You would think of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.780
N choices for your first item leaving you&nbsp;
with N minus one choices for the next item,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.960
leaving you with N minus two choices for the third&nbsp;
item and N minus three choices for the last item.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:30.300
That however, would grossly overcount the&nbsp;
total number since all the different ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:34.440
you can permute these four items would be&nbsp;
counted separately. To account for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.140
you divide out by the extent&nbsp;
to which you've overcounted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:41.340
the number of permutations of four&nbsp;
items which looks like four factorial.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.940
For example, if you calculate 4 choose 4,&nbsp;
everything cancels and you just get one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:53.640
indeed, there is a single intersection point&nbsp;
in this diagram. If you calculate 6 choose 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:59.160
it works out to be 15 and if look at&nbsp;
this diagram and you count them all up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.120
you would notice there are indeed&nbsp;
15 different intersection points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.520
and even if you would never want to count it up&nbsp;
by hand, if we had a diagram that has 100 distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:13.440
points on the exterior and we drew all of the&nbsp;
connecting lines you can conclude that there have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.900
to be 100 choose 4 or just about four million&nbsp;
intersection points somewhere in the middle.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.420
You probably guessed this but these are more than&nbsp;
just warm up questions they give us the necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:28.980
information to answer the question we care about&nbsp;
- how many regions has the circle been cut into?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.960
The trick is to use a very nice little fact&nbsp;
about Planer Graphs. Here I'm using the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.000
"graph" in the sense of a diagram that has nodes&nbsp;
and lines connecting them and what it means to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.680
planar is that you can draw this diagram without&nbsp;
any of the lines intersecting with each other.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.880
In graph theory lingo, you typically&nbsp;
call those nodes "Vertices" and the lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.020
connecting them "Edges" and the wonderful&nbsp;
fact that we can leverage is that if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.280
count up the number of vertices and then&nbsp;
you subtract the total number of edges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.680
and then you consider the number of regions that&nbsp;
this graph has cut the plane into, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.440
that infinite outer region, then always no matter&nbsp;
what plane or graph you started with you get two.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.200
It's actually very fun try this for yourself. Draw&nbsp;
any graph, make sure the lines don't intersect and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.820
then count the number of vertices, subtract&nbsp;
the number of edges and count the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:23.520
regions that it's cut the plane into and no matter&nbsp;
what diagram you chose, the answer always works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:31.500
out to be two. More common you would see this&nbsp;
written as V - E + F = 2 since originally, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:36.960
equation described the vertices, edges and faces&nbsp;
of three dimensional polyhedra and if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:42.180
know why this magical fact is true, you can think&nbsp;
about building up your graph from a trivial case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:48.900
where you have a single node and no edges. So, V&nbsp;
would be equal to one, F would also be equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:54.000
one because of that infinite outer region and&nbsp;
E is zero so the equation is obviously true.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.860
Then if you build up your graph one edge at&nbsp;
a time, one thing that could happen is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:05.220
for each new edge, you introduce a new vertex.&nbsp;
So, E goes up by one but V also goes up by one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.860
leaving the equation balanced. But if a new edge&nbsp;
doesn't correspond to a new vertex, meaning it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:16.500
connecting to a pre-existing vertex, that means&nbsp;
that it's enclosed a new region of space. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.840
E goes up by one but F also goes up by one&nbsp;
which again leaves the equation balanced.

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:29.940
So, as you build up some potentially complicated&nbsp;
graph, V - E + F always stays fixed at two. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:34.080
equation has a name, it's called "Euler's&nbsp;
Characteristic Formula and I remember when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:37.740
I made a video about this a while ago, I&nbsp;
had some dumb joke in there about Euler's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.640
being German for beautiful and there were&nbsp;
a decent number of comments that were like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.780
"You know, Euler is actually a person. I&nbsp;
speak German and it doesn't mean beautiful."

00:08:46.380 --> 00:08:52.020
Anyway, for our purposes, it gives us a tool for&nbsp;
counting the number of regions that a planar graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:57.060
has cut space into. Rearranging a little, you&nbsp;
would take the number of edges minus the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:02.580
of vertices plus two. This is exactly the tool&nbsp;
that we want to understand our circle question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.080
although in that case we don't care about the&nbsp;
infinite outer region. So, instead I'll write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:13.260
this as E - V + 1. And at first you might&nbsp;
complain "but we can't use Euler's formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.940
in this case because it only applies to planar&nbsp;
graphs. And in our case, the lines absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.660
intersect with each other. We even counted&nbsp;
how many times they intersect with each other.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.400
But the key is to treat this as a new graph&nbsp;
where those intersection points are them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:33.000
vertices. So the total number of vertices here&nbsp;
would not be N but N plus N choose four total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:37.620
intersection points. That in turn chops up&nbsp;
all of our chords into a larger number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:42.360
edges. It's much more than just N choose two&nbsp;
and initially, it might seem really annoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.500
and tricky to think about exactly how much&nbsp;
it's chopped them up. But one way you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:51.660
think about it is that every intersection point&nbsp;
takes what started as two separate lines and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:57.600
then turns it into four lines. So, in effect,&nbsp;
each intersection point adds two more edges.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.900
For example, look at this simple diagram where&nbsp;
we have three lines and two intersection points.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.920
The total number of edges after the&nbsp;
chopping would look like three plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.200
two times two or seven. If you had four lines&nbsp;
that intersected at three separate points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:19.380
then the total number of little lines after&nbsp;
chopping would be four plus two times three or 10.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:25.320
And for the diagram we care about where we started&nbsp;
off with N choose two separate lines and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:30.840
chopped up at N choose 4 points in the middle,&nbsp;
you would end up with N choose 2 plus two times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:35.760
N choose 4 edges and actually there are few more&nbsp;
than that. Because we are including the circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.560
we also need to count the N different arcs&nbsp;
that sit on the outside of this diagram.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:46.440
So, with all of that, you have the information you&nbsp;
need to answer the original question. Pulling up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:50.640
our variant of Euler's formula that counts the&nbsp;
number of regions, we'll plug in the expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.380
for the number of vertices which is N plus&nbsp;
the N choose 4 intersection points and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.880
also plug in the slightly larger expression&nbsp;
for the new number of edges, N choose 2 plus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:04.560
2 times N choose 4 plus N and the expression&nbsp;
has a lot of nice cancellation for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.720
you are adding an N but also subtracting an N&nbsp;
and you're adding two copies of N choose 4 but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:14.220
subtracting another copy of N choose 4 and when&nbsp;
all the dust settles, the answer to the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:21.420
is 1 plus N choose 2 plus N choose 4. On the one&nbsp;
hand, you're done. You've answered the question.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:26.700
I give you one of these circle diagrams with N&nbsp;
points on the boundary and using this formula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:30.960
you can figure out how many regions the&nbsp;
circle has been cut into. But of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:34.860
we are not really done because that doesn't&nbsp;
scratch the itch. Why is it the case that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:39.480
this looks like powers of two and then&nbsp;
fall short by just one. It's not just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:43.680
coincidence and the key to answering&nbsp;
it is to consider Pascal's triangle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:48.060
You know this triangle, it's the one where each&nbsp;
term looks like a sum of the two different terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:52.140
above it. And there are basically two facts&nbsp;
we need to bring in about this triangle.

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:57.840
The first is that every term inside this&nbsp;
triangle looks like N choose K for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:02.940
value of N and K. That is the answer to&nbsp;
the question of how many ways can you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:08.280
select a subset of size K from a set of&nbsp;
size N is visible within this triangle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.300
The way to think about it is by indexing&nbsp;
the rows and columns starting from zero.

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:15.540
For example, if you count up to&nbsp;
the zero, one, two, three, four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:22.260
fifth row and you count N to the zero, one,&nbsp;
two, third element, you see ten. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:27.600
5 choose 3 is equal to ten. If you've never seen&nbsp;
this before and you want to know why it's true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.800
there's a really lovely argument, I'll&nbsp;
leave it up as an exercise. But moving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.180
on to the second thing that we need to know,&nbsp;
notice what happens when you add up the rows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:42.360
of this triangle. You get one and then one plus&nbsp;
one is two, one plus two plus one is four. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:47.400
plus three plus three plus one is eight. And as&nbsp;
you continue on, you always get powers of two.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:51.720
Maybe at this point, you're little gun shy&nbsp;
about jumping to conclusions about powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.920
of two too quickly. But in this case, it's a&nbsp;
genuine pattern, there is no trickery being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:00.240
pulled. And there are a few ways that you can&nbsp;
think about why there should be powers of two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:05.040
here. But one that I like is to think about how&nbsp;
as you go from that first row to the next one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:09.900
it's like the number one is sort of donating&nbsp;
two copies of itself into the next row.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.940
Likewise, as you go from the second row to&nbsp;
the third each of those ones is donating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:19.740
two copies of itself to the next row. And in&nbsp;
general as you go from one row to the next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:25.620
each number donates two copies of itself&nbsp;
to the one below. So, as you up the totals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.820
for each of these rows, it stands to reason&nbsp;
that those totals double on each iteration.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:35.760
Circling back to our original question, think&nbsp;
about what this means. The answer to our question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:42.540
looked like 1 plus N choose 2 plus N choose 4. In&nbsp;
the context of Pascal's triangle, you could think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:48.600
about that as adding up the zeroth, second, and&nbsp;
fourth terms inside some row of that triangle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:53.760
For instance, when N is equal to five,&nbsp;
it looks like adding up 1 plus 10 plus 5.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:59.220
Now, because each of those numbers is the sum&nbsp;
of the two above it, this is the same thing as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:04.680
adding up the first five elements in the previous&nbsp;
row, which in this case is adding up the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:09.720
previous row hence why it's a power of two. Same&nbsp;
deal for all the numbers that are five or less.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:15.180
When you situate this formula inside Pascal's&nbsp;
triangle and you relate it to the previous row,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:20.580
what you're doing is adding up the entirety&nbsp;
of that previous row. The point at which this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.580 --> 00:14:25.800
breaks is for N equals 6 because in that case,&nbsp;
when you relate this to the previous row adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:31.200
up the first five elements of that row, it doesn't&nbsp;
cover the whole thing. It falls short specifically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:36.960
by just one which is why we miss the power of two&nbsp;
and why it falls short specifically by just one.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.060
Also, notice what happens when we plug in&nbsp;
N equals 10, looking down at the 10th row&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:46.800
and relating those terms to the previous one&nbsp;
adding the first five elements of the ninth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:52.380
row is exactly half of that row and because the&nbsp;
triangle is symmetric, this means that when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:57.660
add them up, you exactly half of a power of two&nbsp;
which itself of course is another power of two.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:01.980
And as a challenge problem for you, I&nbsp;
don't actually know if this is the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.400
time that you'll ever see a power of&nbsp;
two. Maybe one of you out there who's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.180
clever with diophantine equations than&nbsp;
I am can come up with some clever proof.

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:16.260
Stepping back to summarize, we started by counting&nbsp;
the total number of chords and the total number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:21.300
of intersection points which by thinking about&nbsp;
the right associations is the same as computing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:27.120
N choose 2 and N choose 4. Bringing in Euler's&nbsp;
formula, this let us get an exact closed form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:32.280
expression for the number of regions inside&nbsp;
the circle. Then connecting that with Pascal's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:37.740
triangle gives us a very visceral connection with&nbsp;
the powers of two and why they break when they do.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:43.980
So yes, Moser's Circle Problem is a&nbsp;
cautionary tale about being wary of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:48.900
patterns without proof but at a deeper level,&nbsp;
it also tells us that what sometimes chalked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:52.680
up to be coincidence still leaves&nbsp;
room for satisfying understandings.

